      "text_raw": "A new zero-temperature\nV emerges, which is the\ng00\nextrapolated value, as\nshown in this slide. It is\nalways larger than V . For\ng0\na constant current (m=0),\nits value is about 1.268 V.\nIt is also clear that the\ncurvature is a complicated\nfunction of temperature but\nvery much resembles a\nsecond-order function, a\nparabola. Its largest value is\na zero Kelvin, and is 112 mV\nif m=0. It is even smaller if\nwe allow the current to have\na larger value of m.\nThis curvature is sketched separately on the next slide.",
      "text": "A new zero-temperature V emerges, which is the g00 extrapolated value, as shown in this slide. It is always larger than V . For g0 a constant current (m=0), its value is about 1.268 V. It is also clear that the curvature is a complicated function of temperature but very much resembles a second-order function, a parabola. Its largest value is a zero Kelvin, and is 112 mV if m=0. It is even smaller if we allow the current to have a larger value of m. This curvature is sketched separately on the next slide."
